module dimensioned_arguments

// dimensioned_arguments.ri — IDIOM: every length-semantic geometry argument
// carries a unit. A bare number in a length slot is REJECTED, never read as
// metres and never read as millimetres.
//
//     let plate   = box(20mm, 20mm, plate_h)      // not box(20, 20, 10)
//     let centred = translate(plate, 0mm, 0mm, -plate_h / 2)
//     let twice   = scale(plate, 2)               // dimensionless slot: BARE
//
// ANTI-PATTERN this replaces:
//
//     let plate = box(20, 20, 10)     // don't — written hoping bare means mm
//
// WHY THE REJECTION IS THE FEATURE. There is exactly one sane thing a bare
// number could silently mean, and it is the SI base unit: metres. So a `20`
// written by an author who meant `20mm` would build a 20-METRE plate — a 1000x
// scale error that nothing downstream catches, because the model stays
// perfectly self-consistent at the wrong size. `scale_error` below makes that
// factor executable rather than rhetorical: it is `20m / 20mm`, pinned at 1000.
//
// WHAT LEGITIMATELY STAYS BARE. Not every geometry argument is a length.
// Unit-vector and axis-direction components, `scale` factors, repeat counts,
// NURBS weights and indices are dimensionless by construction, and a unit on
// one of them says something the author does not mean. That half is an
// authoring rule you uphold, NOT one the compiler enforces — the gate runs in
// one direction only, so `scale(g, 2mm)` is accepted. `twice` below is the
// idiom written the way you mean it.
//
// WHERE THE TABLES LIVE — deliberately NOT restated here. The rejected-form /
// migration table, the per-argument diagnostic wording (the
// `pass a dimensioned length such as `5mm`` tail is
// `reify-core::units::LENGTH_MIGRATION_HINT`), the bare-`0`-is-not-special-cased
// case, and the `reify check` EXIT-CODE split all live in one place:
// `crates/reify-mcp/src/tools/chunks/units.md`, section
// "Dimensioned Geometry Arguments" — topic `units` of `reify_language_reference`.
// That section's tables are gated byte-exactly by
// `crates/reify-compiler/tests/harness_doc_chunks/units_chunk_smoke.rs`, which
// runs the real compiler over both columns. Re-quoting them here would be a
// second copy to keep in sync — exactly the rot this corpus exists to avoid.
// Which ARGUMENT of which constructor is length-semantic is catalogued per
// position in the `geometry` chunk, under "Dimensioned arguments".
//
// THE ONE THING WORTH CARRYING HERE, because it decides how you gate a design:
// `reify eval` and `reify build` reject every bare number in a length ARGUMENT
// slot, exit 1, every time. `reify check` is NOT equivalent — it prints the
// same `error:` lines, but its exit code depends on which layer owns it: 1 for
// the compile-layer constructors (`box`, `translate`, `fillet`, the 7-arg
// `mirror` pivot), 0 for the eval-only ones (`helix`, `polygon`, ...) even
// though the errors are right there on stderr. Measured 2026-09-07. So read
// check's STDERR, and gate on `reify eval`, never on check's exit status alone.
//
// Verified contracts:
//
//   ONE LENGTH, THREE SPELLINGS. `20mm`, `2cm` and `0.02m` are the same value,
//   not three approximations of it — all three converge on the identical f64,
//   which is why the two equalities below are EXACT `==` rather than the bands
//   the rest of this file uses. A unit suffix is a conversion at parse time,
//   not a tag carried alongside a number.
//
//   THE 1000x, MADE EXECUTABLE. `bare_reading` is what a bare `20` WOULD have
//   meant had the compiler accepted it, spelled explicitly as `20m`.
//   `scale_error = bare_reading / plate_w` is dimensionless and pinned in a
//   999..1001 band, so the magnitude of the mistake is asserted by the
//   constraint gate rather than asserted in this comment.
//
//   DIMENSIONED ARITHMETIC COMPOSES. A dimensionless ratio times a Length is a
//   Length (`web_t = web_frac * plate_h`), and a Length divided by a bare
//   number stays a Length — which is why the hand-centring idiom needs no extra
//   unit on `-plate_h / 2`. Honest scope on the `: Length` annotations below,
//   which are easy to over-read: over a bare LITERAL an annotation is
//   documentation, not a gate — `let w : Length = 2` evaluates to `2` and exits
//   0 with no diagnostic, and the `param` spelling only warns. What makes these
//   bindings load-bearing is that something dimensioned CONSUMES them: respell
//   `2cm` as a bare `2` and it is the equality constraint that fails —
//   `error: incompatible types in comparison: Scalar[m] vs Int`, exit 1 under
//   both `check` and `eval`. The annotation itself bites when the initializer
//   is an EXPRESSION whose type disagrees with it, which is `web_t` below:
//   `web_frac * 10` in its place is rejected at the binding rather than
//   downstream. Measured 2026-09-08.
//
// Bands, not sign guards: `web_t > 0mm` would survive a regression that
// preserved dimensions but moved magnitudes. These bands fail instead.

structure def DimensionedArguments {
    param plate_w  : Length = 20mm
    param plate_h  : Length = 10mm
    param web_frac : Real   = 0.4

    // One length, three spellings — exact, not approximate.
    let w_cm : Length = 2cm
    let w_m  : Length = 0.02m

    // What a bare `20` would have meant, had it been accepted.
    let bare_reading : Length = 20m
    let scale_error  = bare_reading / plate_w      // 1000, dimensionless

    // Ratio * Length -> Length. The annotation is the assertion.
    let web_t : Length = web_frac * plate_h        // 0.004 m

    // THE IDIOM: every length slot carries a unit...
    let plate   = box(plate_w, plate_w, plate_h)
    let centred = translate(plate, 0mm, 0mm, -plate_h / 2)   // Length/bare stays Length

    // ...and every dimensionless slot stays bare. `scale`'s factor is a
    // multiplier, not a length; `2mm` here would be accepted and would mean
    // nothing.
    let twice = scale(plate, 2)

    // Exact: all three spellings are the same f64.
    constraint plate_w == w_cm
    constraint plate_w == w_m

    // The 1000x that a silently-accepted bare `20` would have cost.
    constraint scale_error > 999.0
    constraint scale_error < 1001.0

    // Derived length keeps its dimension and its magnitude.
    constraint web_t > 3.9mm
    constraint web_t < 4.1mm

    // A ratio is a ratio — no unit belongs on it.
    constraint web_frac > 0.0
    constraint web_frac < 1.0
}
